\details{
Briefly, the standard GEE predictor is based on the marginal mean model alone, i.e.,

\deqn{
 \mu_{i} = h^{-1}(X_{\alpha} \alpha_{g_i} + X_{\beta} \beta_i),
}

where \eqn{\alpha_{g_i}} denotes the vector of coefficients for the assigned species archetype, and \eqn{\beta_i} is the coefficient vector specific to species \eqn{i}. The species-specific component should generally contain the intercept and any covariates whose effects are not intended to characterize the species archetypes. Finally, \eqn{h^{-1}} denotes the inverse link function of the specified family. The adjusted GEE predictor views the GEE as solving an iterative working linear model, and borrows ideas from universal kriging to exploit the \emph{working cross-correlations} between the observed sites and the new sites. That is, for species i, we have

\deqn{
\mu_{adj,i} = \mu_i + A_{i,new}^{1/2} R_{curr,new}^\top R_{curr,curr}^{-1} r_{i,curr},
}
where \eqn{r_{i,curr}} denote the Pearson residuals of species i, \eqn{R_{curr,curr}} is the working correlation matrix for current (training) data only, \eqn{R_{curr,new}} is the working cross-correlation matrix between current and new sites, and \eqn{A_{i,new}} is the diagonal matrix of marginal variances for species i at the new sites. Adjusted GEE predictions are available for all cases except \code{object$working_correlation_control$cor_type = "supplied"}. Of course if \code{cor_type = "independence"} then the cross-correlations are zero and the adjusted and standard GEE predictions coincide.

Uncertainty (Wald-type) intervals for standard and adjusted GEE predictions are also provided, and we refer to Hui, Muller and Welsh (2025) for details on the construction of the adjusted GEE intervals. If an adjusted GEE prediction falls outside the valid range of the mean, then it is reverted to the standard GEE prediction. Moreover, for all uncertainty intervals, truncation is applied to the limits as appropriate to ensure they stay within the range of the response.

Note the archetype assignments are treated as fixed, so uncertainty in the species groupings is not accounted for.
}
